@startuml D3_Flow2_CLAUDEmd_System
' ============================================================
' DOMAIN 3 — FLOW 2: The CLAUDE.md System
' Hierarchy + Precedence + Subdirectory + Exclusion +
' Instruction Design + Team Patterns  (Topics 6–11)
' CORE TRAP 1: load ORDER is NOT a precedence rule — genuine
'   CLAUDE.md conflicts are NON-DETERMINISTIC.
' CORE TRAP 2: for settings.json PERMISSIONS, layers MERGE and
'   DENY beats ALLOW from ANY layer (even user).
' ============================================================
title Domain 3 · Flow 2 — The CLAUDE.md System (T6–11)\n(4 scopes concatenated · conflicts = non-deterministic · deny always wins)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12
skinparam activityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF6CC

start

' ---------- TOPIC 6: FOUR SCOPES ----------
partition "T6 · The 4 CLAUDE.md Scopes (loaded broad->narrow, ALL AT ONCE)" {
  :1. MANAGED POLICY — org-wide, system path;
  note right
    * Loads FIRST · CANNOT be excluded
    * Deployed centrally by IT / device mgmt
    * The ONLY real ENFORCEMENT mechanism
    * OUTSIDE the repo
    e.g. "never log customer PII"
  end note
  :2. USER SCOPE — ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md;
  note right
    * Personal prefs across ALL your projects
    * NEVER shared, by design (not in git)
    * OUTSIDE the repo
    e.g. "short answers, no emoji"
  end note
  :3. PROJECT SCOPE — CLAUDE.md at repo root;
  note right
    * VERSION-CONTROLLED / committed
    * Every contributor gets it IDENTICALLY
    * INSIDE the repo (the shared one)
    e.g. "spaces not tabs, every fn needs a test"
  end note
  :4. LOCAL SCOPE — CLAUDE.local.md;
  note right
    * In the repo but GITIGNORED
    * Personal tweaks for ONE project
    * INSIDE the repo (not committed)
    e.g. "verbose debug output for me only"
  end note
  #LightCyan:All 4 CONCATENATED into one instruction set\n— nothing is erased;
}

' ---------- TOPIC 7: PRECEDENCE / CONFLICT (the big trap) ----------
partition "T7 · Precedence & Conflict Resolution" {
  if (Do two instructions genuinely CONFLICT?) then (YES)
    #Pink:NON-DETERMINISTIC — Claude may follow EITHER;
    note right
      == THE BIG TRAP ==
      Load ORDER is NOT a precedence rule.
      You CANNOT count on closer/more-specific winning.
      e.g. user says semicolons, project says none -> unpredictable.
      FIX: don't write the conflict. If overlap is possible,
      state precedence EXPLICITLY in the MORE SPECIFIC file
      (project/local) — never in user (invisible to teammates).
    end note
  else (NO — complementary)
    #LightGreen:BOTH simply apply (the common case);
    note left
      user "be concise" + project "always add type hints"
      -> both apply. Overlap resolution is the EXCEPTION.
    end note
  endif
  :Verify what's loaded:\n`/memory` lists files · `/context` inspects loaded;
  note right
    No automatic startup printout of loaded files.
    An "instructions loaded" hook can log it if you want.
    ----
    CLAUDE.md vs AUTO-MEMORY:
    CLAUDE.md = hand-authored config, loaded fresh each
      session, counts vs window, never self-edits.
    Auto-memory = separate store in home dir, keyed to the
      git repo, stays on your machine, accumulates on its own.
    "Treat CLAUDE.md like a config file, not a journal."
  end note
}

floating note right
  == TWO KILLER SCOPE TRAPS ==
  1. Org-wide enforcement = MANAGED POLICY ONLY.
     Putting "never commit secrets" in ONE project's
     CLAUDE.md leaves 40 other repos uncovered.
     Importing/symlinking = SHARING, not ENFORCING.
  2. Team standards in USER config = the silent killer.
     "always write tests" in YOUR user file works on
     YOUR machine; teammate's machine loads THEIR user
     file (no such rule) -> drift, no error, no warning.
     FIX: team needs it -> committed PROJECT CLAUDE.md.
  ----
  Audience test: whole company -> managed;
  just me, everywhere -> user; the team/repo -> project.
end note

' ---------- TOPIC 8: SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md ----------
partition "T8 · Subdirectory CLAUDE.md (scoped, LAZY-loaded)" {
  :Drop a CLAUDE.md inside a subfolder (e.g. /frontend);
  if (Does Claude READ a file inside that folder?) then (YES)
    #LightGreen:Subdir CLAUDE.md loads NOW (lazy) and ADDS to root;
    note right
      == 2 EXAM POINTS ==
      1. It ADDS to root config, never REPLACES it.
         Parent rules stay active; subdir file read LAST —
         but read-last does NOT guarantee winning
         (contradiction -> non-deterministic again).
      2. No registration/manifest — PLACEMENT is the scope.
         Audit rules just by where files live.
      e.g. /frontend: hooks start "use", PascalCase components,
      co-located tests. Invisible while in /infra.
    end note
  else (NO — never read a file there)
    #LightGray:Subdir file stays OUT of context;
  endif
}

' ---------- TOPIC 9: EXCLUDING FILES (settings.json permissions) ----------
partition "T9 · Excluding Files & Content (settings.json)" {
  :Write READ DENY entries in settings.json (glob syntax);
  note right
    Real mechanism = read deny rule in settings.json.
    Glob: one * = one path segment; ** = across dirs.
    Highest-value: deny read of .env, .env.*, secrets/**,
    ~/.ssh, cloud cred folders.
  end note
  #LightCyan:PERMISSION rules MERGE across all 4 setting layers;
  note right
    ============================================
    PERMISSIONS ≠ ordinary settings:
    * Ordinary setting (model, theme): higher layer
      OVERRIDES lower (managed>local>project>user). One wins.
    * PERMISSION rules: MERGE across layers, and
      DENY is evaluated BEFORE allow.
      -> a DENY in ANY layer (even user) blocks an
         ALLOW in managed. DENY ALWAYS WINS.
    (This is settings.json + DETERMINISTIC —
     different from CLAUDE.md's non-deterministic conflicts.)
    ============================================
  end note
  :Know the BOUNDARY of deny;
  note right
    Deny blocks Claude's OWN tools: read, edit, @-mention,
    shell reads (cat/head). It does NOT block an arbitrary
    SUBPROCESS — a Python script Claude runs can still open
    the file. For real enforcement -> SANDBOXING.
    ----
    TWO MYTHS TO RETIRE:
    * No .claudeignore file exists — never has.
    * Claude Code does NOT respect .gitignore —
      ignored files are fully readable unless denied.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 10: INSTRUCTION DESIGN ----------
partition "T10 · Instruction Design (what actually gets followed)" {
  :Write each directive: SPECIFIC · ACTIONABLE · UNAMBIGUOUS;
  note right
    NOT "use consistent naming"
    BUT "all module files use snake_case".
    If you can't VERIFY it was followed, rewrite it.
    Concise bullets are followed more reliably than
    prose buried in paragraphs (and cost fewer tokens).
    Mental model: a STYLE-GUIDE CHECKLIST, not a design doc.
  end note
  if (Can Claude know this ONLY if you tell it?) then (YES = repo-specific)
    #LightGreen:ENCODE it;
    note left
      Encode: language/runtime versions, style conventions,
      test requirements, commit format, team process,
      known anti-patterns, security rules.
      Value = stop re-explaining + stop re-suggesting
      what you already ruled out. Decisions, not education.
    end note
  else (NO = general knowledge / linter's job)
    #Pink:SKIP it;
    note right
      Skip: general programming, stdlib behaviour,
      anything the linter/formatter already enforces
      (Black handles format -> no format rules).
      Test: delete the line — would behaviour change
      in a way that matters? No -> cut.
    end note
  endif
}

' ---------- TOPIC 11: TEAM PATTERNS ----------
partition "T11 · Common CLAUDE.md Patterns for Real Teams" {
  :Apply the right pattern per context;
  note right
    Front-end: component naming, CSS method, state mgmt, a11y
    Back-end : API design, DB/ORM policy, error format
    Full-stack/monorepo: shared types, import limits, boundaries
    SECURITY (every project): no hard-coded creds, secrets via
      env/manager, .env commit policy, approved auth —
      place EARLY & prominently so it survives context pressure.
    ----
    2 ANTI-PATTERNS:
    a. NARRATIVE CLAUDE.md — rules buried in prose -> missed
       under pressure. Use bullets, one rule per line.
    b. TOOL DUPLICATION — re-encoding what Black/ESLint already
       enforce -> a 2nd source of truth that DRIFTS.
    Rule: let tools enforce what tools can; CLAUDE.md fills gaps.
  end note
}

stop

floating note left
  == FLOW 2 SPINE ==
  4 scopes concatenated (broad->narrow) -> conflicts in
  CLAUDE.md are NON-DETERMINISTIC -> subdir files ADD & load
  lazily -> exclusion is settings.json DENY (merges, deny wins)
  -> write specific/verifiable directives -> encode only
  repo-specific decisions -> apply team patterns, avoid the
  narrative & tool-duplication anti-patterns.
  ----
  == DON'T CONFUSE THE TWO CONFLICT RULES ==
  CLAUDE.md (natural language)  -> non-deterministic
  settings.json permissions     -> deterministic, DENY wins
end note

@enduml
